        "speaker": "client",
        "text": "The six points should be handled as follows:\n\n1\\. Site list\nFor the model, treat the Business Case site list as final.\n\n2\\. Opex figures\nThe correct program-level operating assumption is the original brief's CAD 48–64M/year range. Because the Appendix amount appears incomplete or understated, it must not be used for the full-network model.\n\n3\\. Availability payment approach\nSize the availability payment to meet both of these targets:\n\n- A minimum DSCR of ~1.65x; and\n\n- A minimum equity IRR in the 16–20% range.\n\nIf relying on DSCR alone produces negative equity returns, the model must not use that approach.\n\n4\\. Program-wide/shared costs\nDigital-platform, program-management, and contingency/reserve costs should appear as separate line items within total program capex rather than being excluded.\n\n5\\. Financing structure\nFor this stage, retain the streamlined 55/45 debt/equity structure at ~9%. There is no need to add a separate mezzanine layer yet; we can refine the financing after engaging lenders.\n\n6\\. Tax/depreciation\nInstead of the flat tax treatment, model CCA/depreciation properly using the applicable standard Canadian Capital Cost Allowance classes for each asset type—for example, Class 6 for buildings, Class 50 for clean energy equipment, and Class 12 for furniture/fixtures. Proper treatment is essential to a credible government submission because it will materially affect both the tax shield and equity returns.\n\nThe immediate priority is to produce a coherent base-case model quickly and then iterate on it."
      },
